                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To row a boat using a scull or sculls."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "36fb2e73-44ce-44e4-a15d-48c0c4728c23",
                                                "sense_id": "1653c256-6b93-372c-9c10-0ec9a597acb2",
                                                "text": "The afternoon sun was getting low as the Rat sculled gently homewards in a dreamy mood, murmuring poetry-things over to himself, and not paying much attention to Mole."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bf77bf00-9731-3f61-96f6-dcb217ef6366",
                                        "entry_id": "0847e8ca-c00b-3209-b6d0-a0fdbd93c0e6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To skate while keeping both feet in contact with the ground or ice."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "scull",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0b7a9ba3-6866-35ca-8b72-2ad55a9a5163",
                                        "entry_id": "fb5c6e17-ca67-3675-b628-4692443b04e2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Archaic spelling of skull."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "archaic"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ce326eff-e98e-4670-a4f8-4d44e65251d8",
                                                "sense_id": "0b7a9ba3-6866-35ca-8b72-2ad55a9a5163",
                                                "text": "The sculls were so tender, that they generally fell to pieces on being touched. The other bones were stronger. There were some teeth which were judged to be smaller than those of an adult; a scull which on a slight view, appeared to be that of an infant, […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1891c189-a5ca-3be9-865d-e4ba4f0ac71a",
                                        "entry_id": "fb5c6e17-ca67-3675-b628-4692443b04e2",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A skull cap. A small bowl-shaped helmet, without visor or bever."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2732175d-7bb4-4ed5-b584-4ea43d3f2c99",
                                                "sense_id": "1891c189-a5ca-3be9-865d-e4ba4f0ac71a",
                                                "text": "The scull is a head piece, without visor or bever, resembling a bowl or bason, such as was worn by our cavalry, within twenty or thirty years."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "scull",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                        "entry_id": "a3f042ff-0def-361a-b03e-c5ec78c5a497",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To drink the entire contents of a drinking vessel without pausing."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "New-Zealand",
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3b25e8d4-197f-43bf-b3f9-f4be1ba0a7c4",
                                                "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                                "text": "2005, Jane Egginton, Working and Living Australia, The Sunday Times, Cadogan Guides, UK, page 59,\nIn 1954, Bob Hawke made the Guinness Book of Records for sculling 2.5 pints of beer in 11 seconds."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8c5e53cf-108d-4618-be9c-bc875be56556",
                                                "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                                "text": "That way you get your opponent so gassed up from sculling beer that all he can think about is trying to burp without spewing."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "722e913f-c912-495d-a61f-d2b12e4e45dc",
                                                "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                                "text": "For a livelier scene, head here on Friday or Saturday night, when mass beer-sculling (chugging) and yodeling are accompanied by a brass band and costumed waitresses ferrying foaming beer steins about the atmospheric, cellarlike space."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0f8f8085-d93d-45f4-a1e0-ff3b7b773bbe",
                                                "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                                "text": "After a three-day Torquay-to-Sydney road trip with his hosts, Noll rejoined his American temmates, unshaven and stinking of alcohol, the Team USA badge ripped from his warm-up jacket and replaced by an Aussie-made patch of Disney character Gladstone Gander sculling a frothy mug of beer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b2585477-b0f5-425a-a968-b91223e02063",
                                                "sense_id": "098bde06-1182-3660-b0f9-45b0e0e2f8ec",
                                                "text": "I sipped it. It was thick and sweet and yuck. It went somewhere and did something I couldn't pinpoint. I sculled the rest."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "scull",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
